    <div class="main-grid grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_240px] gap-10">
      <main class="min-w-0">
        <div class="ad-slot w-full h-24 mb-8 flex items-center justify-center border border-dashed border-gray-300 rounded-lg bg-gray-50"><span class="text-gray-400 text-xs font-mono tracking-wider">ADVERTISEMENT</span></div>
        <article class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden">
          <header class="px-6 sm:px-10 pt-10 pb-8 border-b border-gray-100">
            <div class="animate-fade-up">
              <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-5">
                <span class="text-blue-600 font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-[11px] bg-blue-50 px-3 py-1 rounded-full">Deep Dive</span>
                <span class="text-gray-400 text-xs">12 min read</span>
              </div>
              <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-[2.65rem] font-extrabold leading-[1.15] tracking-tight text-gray-900 mb-5">RWA Tokenization: What's Actually Happening in <span class="text-blue-600">2025</span> (And What's Just Hype)</h1>
              <p class="text-lg text-gray-500 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-6">Everyone's talking about BlackRock tokenizing Treasuries and Ondo building yield stablecoins. We stopped reading Twitter takes and looked at the <strong class="text-gray-700">actual on-chain numbers</strong>. Here's what we found — and what the crypto narrative gets wrong.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="animate-fade-up animate-fade-up-delay-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 pt-5 border-t border-gray-100">
              <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-500 to-indigo-600 flex items-center justify-center text-white font-bold text-sm">R</div>
                <div><p class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-800">RWAHQ Editorial</p><p class="text-xs text-gray-400">Independent Research</p></div>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-center gap-3 text-xs text-gray-400 ml-auto">
                <time datetime="2025-06-02" class="flex items-center gap-1"><i data-lucide="calendar" class="w-3.5 h-3.5"></i> Jun 2, 2025</time>
                <span class="flex items-center gap-1"><i data-lucide="eye" class="w-3.5 h-3.5"></i> 12.4K</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </header>
          <div class="prose-custom px-6 sm:px-10 py-10">
            <blockquote class="not-prose border-l-4 border-blue-500 pl-5 py-2 my-8 bg-blue-50/50 rounded-r-lg">
              <p class="text-lg font-medium text-gray-700 italic leading-relaxed">"Every conference in 2024 had a panel about tokenization. Every crypto Twitter thread mentioned BlackRock. But when you actually look at the numbers, something doesn't add up — and that something matters more than the hype suggests."</p>
            </blockquote>

            <h2 id="the-liquidity-problem" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-2 mb-4">Let's Start With the Actual Problem</h2>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Here's a number that gets thrown around a lot: <strong>$280 trillion in illiquid assets</strong>. The implication is usually that all of this is just waiting to be tokenized. But that's not really what's happening.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The truth is more boring. Most of those "illiquid" assets are illiquid for structural reasons that tokenization doesn't fully solve. Commercial real estate is slow to sell because buyers need to do due diligence, not because the settlement takes too long. Private credit depends on relationship networks that don't exist on-chain. Physical gold in a Swiss vault is actually pretty liquid already — you can sell it within a day through a broker.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">So when we talk about RWA, we need to be specific about <em>which</em> illiquidity problem we're solving. The real opportunity isn't "tokenize everything" — it's <span class="kw-highlight font-medium">tokenizing the specific assets where on-chain access creates genuine new utility</span>: 24/7 secondary markets for things that previously had no secondary market at all.</p>

            <div class="ad-slot my-8 py-4 border-y border-gray-100 flex justify-center"><div class="w-full max-w-[336px] h-[280px] bg-gray-50 flex items-center justify-center border border-gray-200 rounded-lg"><span class="text-gray-400 text-xs font-mono text-center leading-relaxed">ADSENSE / MONETAG<br>(336×280)</span></div></div>

            <h2 id="where-the-numbers-stand" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where the Numbers Actually Stand in 2025</h2>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">According to <a href="https://defillama.com/categories/rwa" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">DeFi Llama's RWA category</a>, the total value locked in tokenized real-world assets sits at roughly <strong>$8.2 billion</strong> as of May 2025. That's up from about $2.1 billion at the end of 2023 — which is impressive growth. But it's also worth context.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">To put that $8.2 billion in perspective: it's roughly 0.003% of that "$280 trillion" figure. Even if you only count the "realistically tokenizable" portion (maybe $10-20 trillion), we're at less than 0.1% penetration. The trend is real, but the narrative that "trillions are moving on-chain" is, frankly, ahead of reality.</p>

            <div class="not-prose grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 my-8">
              <div class="stat-card bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 text-center">
                <p class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-blue-600 font-mono">$8.2B</p>
                <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1.5 uppercase tracking-wider font-medium">Total RWA TVL</p>
                <p class="text-[10px] text-gray-400 mt-1">Source: DeFi Llama, May 2025</p>
              </div>
              <div class="stat-card bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 text-center">
                <p class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-blue-600 font-mono">~4x</p>
                <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1.5 uppercase tracking-wider font-medium">Growth Since 2023</p>
                <p class="text-[10px]text-gray-400 mt-1">From $2.1B to $8.2B</p>
              </div>
              <div class="stat-card bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 text-center">
                <p class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-blue-600 font-mono">$470M</p>
                <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1.5 uppercase tracking-wider font-medium">BlackRock BUIDL</p>
                <p class="text-[10px] text-gray-400 mt-1">Launched March 2024</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="what-actually-works" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What's Actually Working Right Now</h2>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Despite the hype gap, some things genuinely work well. Let's walk through them honestly.</p>

            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-800 mt-8 mb-3">Tokenized US Treasuries — The Clearest Win</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">This is the segment with the strongest product-market fit. BlackRock's <strong>BUIDL fund</strong> holds tokenized US T-Bills and has attracted roughly $470 million in TVL since launching in March 2024. Franklin Templeton's FOBXX on Polygon has similar mechanics. Ondo Finance's OUSG provides exposure to tokenized Treasuries with a DeFi-native wrapper.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">Why this works: T-Bills are standardized, priced in real-time, and the custody infrastructure already exists. Tokenizing them mostly adds instant settlement and 24/7 access. The value proposition is clear — it's not speculative, it's just more efficient plumbing for something that already works.</p>

            <div class="not-prose bg-gradient-to-br from-amber-50 to-yellow-50 border border-amber-200/60 rounded-xl p-6 mb-5">
              <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
                <div class="w-12 h-12 bg-amber-100 rounded-xl flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0"><i data-lucide="landmark" class="w-6 h-6 text-amber-600"></i></div>
                <div>
                  <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">Tokenized Treasuries</h3>
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">BUIDL, FOBXX, and OUSG collectively hold hundreds of millions in tokenized US government debt. The mechanics are boring — and that's exactly why they work. Standardized asset, clear pricing, existing custody. No innovation theater needed.</p>
                  <ul class="text-sm text-gray-600 space-y-1.5">
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><i data-lucide="check-circle-2" class="w-4 h-4 text-amber-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0"></i> Each token backed 1:1 by T-Bills held by BNY Mellon or State Street</li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><i data-lucide="check-circle-2" class="w-4 h-4 text-amber-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0"></i> Yield distributed automatically via smart contracts</li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><i data-lucide="check-circle-2" class="w-4 h-4 text-amber-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0"></i> Redeemable for underlying asset (in theory — in practice, mostly traded on secondary market)</li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-800 mt-8 mb-3">Tokenized Gold — Actually Pretty Mature</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4"><a href="https://defillama.com/protocol/tether-gold" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Tether Gold (XAUT)</a> and <a href="https://defillama.com/protocol/pax-gold" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">PAX Gold (PAXG)</a> have been around since 2019 and collectively hold over $580 million in tokenized gold. The World Gold Council has published reports acknowledging tokenized gold as legitimate. The infrastructure is tested — this isn't experimental anymore.</p>

            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-800 mt-8 mb-3">Real Estate — Where It Gets Complicated</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4"><span class="kw-highlight font-medium">Real estate tokenization is the category where the narrative most diverges from reality.</span> RealT has tokenized hundreds of US rental properties and actually distributes rental yields through smart contracts. That's real and working. But the broader "fractional Manhattan buildings for $10" narrative runs into serious friction.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">The problem isn't technical — it's legal and operational. Who manages the property? What happens during a default? How do you handle tenant disputes on-chain? These aren't problems that better smart contracts solve. They're problems that require real-world legal structures that don't scale the way crypto people assume they do.</p>

            <div class="not-prose bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-xl p-5 my-6 flex items-start gap-3">
              <i data-lucide="alert-triangle" class="w-5 h-5 text-amber-500 flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5"></i>
              <p class="text-sm text-amber-800 leading-relaxed"><strong>Honest take:</strong> Real estate tokenization is real and working at small scale. But the "trillions of real estate coming on-chain" narrative ignores that the bottleneck was never settlement speed — it was legal complexity and buyer due diligence. Tokenization helps at the margins but doesn't restructure how real estate actually trades.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="ad-slot my-8 py-4 border-y border-gray-100 flex justify-center"><div class="w-full max-w-[336px] h-[280px] bg-gray-50 flex items-center justify-center border border-gray-200 rounded-lg"><span class="text-gray-400 text-xs font-mono text-center leading-relaxed">ADSENSE / MONETAG<br>(336×280)</span></div></div>

            <h2 id="why-institutions-care" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Institutions Actually Care (It's Not What You Think)</h2>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The crypto narrative frames institutional RWA adoption as "Wall Street finally sees the light." That's self-serving. The real reasons are more pragmatic.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>1. Operational efficiency, not ideology.</strong> BlackRock didn't launch BUIDL because they became crypto believers. They launched it because running a money market fund on-chain is structurally cheaper than running it through traditional infrastructure. Less manual reconciliation, faster settlement, fewer intermediaries. It's a cost play, not a philosophical one.</p>
            <p class="text="gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>2. Client demand, not top-down strategy.</strong> Institutional clients (pension funds, sovereign wealth funds) started asking for tokenized exposure. BlackRock is responding to demand, not creating it. There's an important difference.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6"><strong>3. Regulatory windows are opening.</strong> The EU's MiCA framework, which took effect in December 2024, created a compliance pathway for tokenized securities. Singapore's Project Guardian continues to expand. Even in the US, the regulatory conversation has shifted from "is this legal?" to "how do we comply?" — which is a materially different starting point.</p>

            <h2 id="risks-everyone-ignores" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Risks That Everyone Likes to Ignore</h2>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i data-lucide="link-2-off" class="w-5 h-5 text-red-500"></i><h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-800">Smart Contract Risk</h3></div>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Every RWA token depends on a smart contract. A bug in that contract could freeze or drain the underlying assets. This risk is small for audited protocols but it's not zero — and it compounds across trillions in notional value.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i data-lucide="scale" class="w-5 h-5 text-red-500"></i><h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-800">Oracle Risk</h3></div>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Token prices depend on oracles (Chainlink, Pyth). If an oracle fails or gets manipulated, the protocol might think your $100M collateral is worth $0. This has happened before. It will happen again.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i data-lucide="lock" class="w-5 h-5 text-red-500"></i><h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-800">Custodian Risk</h3></div>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Your token says it's backed by gold in a vault. But who controls the vault? What happens if the custodian goes bankrupt? BNY Mellon isn't going under anytime soon, but not all custodians have that track record.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><i data-lucide="shield-off" class="w-5 h-5 text-red-500"></i><h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-800">Regulatory Reversal</h3></div>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">MiCA provides clarity in the EU. But what happens if the SEC changes its interpretation? Or if a new administration decides to crack down on "crypto securities"? Regulatory risk is the one thing you can't diversify away with better technology.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="conclusion" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Our Take</h2>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">RWA tokenization is real and growing — but the conversation around it is deeply unserious. People are throwing around "trillions on-chain" projections based on multiplying current TVL by arbitrary penetration rates. That's not analysis. That's wishful thinking.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">What's actually happening is more nuanced. Tokenized Treasuries are a genuine product-market fit and will probably become standard infrastructure. Tokenized gold works and has for years. Real estate tokenization is real at small scale but faces non-technical barriers that the crypto industry consistently underestimates.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-8">The smart move isn't to bet on "RWA going mainstream." It's to understand which specific asset classes benefit from on-chain settlement, which ones don't, and why. Most people doing "RWA research" aren't doing that. We're trying to.</p>

            <div class="not-prose mt-10 p-6 bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-900 to-indigo-900 rounded-xl text-center">
              <p class="text-blue-300 text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] mb-4">Sponsored Content</p>
              <div class="bg-white/10 w-full h-36 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center border border-white/10"><span class="text-white/40 text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wider">Native Banner Ads</span></div>
