            <h2 id="faq" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div class="not-prose space-y-3 mb-8" id="faq-container">
              <div class="faq-item bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden"><button class="faq-toggle w-full flex items-center justify-between p-5 text-left hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors" aria-expanded="false"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-800 pr-4">What is RWA in crypto?</span><i data-lucide="chevron-down" class="faq-chevron w-5 h-5 text-gray-400 flex-shrink-0"></i></button><div class="faq-answer px-5 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed"><div class="pb-5">RWA (Real World Assets) in crypto refers to physical or traditional financial assets represented as tokens on a blockchain. The concept has been around since stablecoins in 2014, but the scope expanded significantly in 2024-2025 as BlackRock, Franklin Templeton, and others launched tokenized funds for Treasuries, gold, and other real-world assets.</div></div></div>
              <div class="faq-item bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden"><button class="faq-toggle w-full flex items-center justify-between p-5 text-left hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors" aria-expanded="false"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-800 pr-4">How big is the RWA market actually?</span><i data-lucide="chevron-down" class="faq-chevron w-5 h-5 text-gray-400 flex-shrink-0"></i></button><div class="faq-answer px-5 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed"><div class="pb-5">According to DeFi Llama, the RWA category reached approximately $8.2 billion in TVL as of May 2025, up from $2.1 billion at end of 2023. That's roughly 4x growth in 18 months. Impressive, but still a tiny fraction of the addressable market. The "trillions on-chain" narrative significantly exaggerates current reality.</div></div></div>
              <div class="faq-item bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden"><button class="faq-toggle w-full flex items-center justify-between p-5 text-left hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors" aria-expanded="false"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-800 pr-4">Is BlackRock's BUIDL actually legit?</span><i data-lucide="chevron-down" class="faq-chevron w-5 h-5 text-gray-400 flex-shrink-0"></i></button><div class="faq-answer px-5 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed"><div class="pb-5">Yes. BUIDL is a registered fund with BNY Mellon as custodian. It holds actual US Treasury bills and issues ERC-20 tokens representing shares. As of May 2025, it holds around $470 million in TVL. It's small relative to BlackRock's $10+ trillion AUM, but it's a real product — not a demo or a concept.</div></div></div>
              <div class="faq-item bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden"><button class="faq-toggle w-full flex items-center justify-between p-5 text-left hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors" aria-expanded="false"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-800 pr-4">Is RWA crypto safe to invest in?</span><i data-lucide="chevron-down" class="faq-chevron w-5 h-5 text-gray-400 flex-shrink-0"></i></button><div class="faq-answer px-5 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed"><div class="pb-5">Tokenization doesn't eliminate underlying asset risk. A tokenized Treasury can still be affected by interest rate changes. A tokenized property can still lose value. Smart contracts can have bugs. What tokenization adds is transparency (you can verify on-chain that the collateral exists), faster settlement, and better accessibility. But the fundamental risk of the asset remains. Treat it like any other investment — not like a crypto moonshot.</div></div></div>
            </div>

